7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The strongest statutory breach on 2 January 2023 remains A. The ESA requires any employer that reaches or exceeds 25 employees to have a written disconnecting-from-work policy in place; the obligation is triggered at the moment headcount crosses the line, with no statutory language limiting the duty exclusively to the prior 1 January snapshot. When the eight new hires’ contracts took effect on 10 January 2023, A’s workforce moved from 20 to 28 employees and no policy existed, creating an immediate violation.  

Colleagues 11 and 12 correctly note that C’s monthly revisions followed by quarterly distribution create at least some 30-day gaps, yet that timing issue is secondary to the outright absence of any mandated policy. C already maintains both required policies for a large, stable workforce, and incremental updates are not the same as a total failure to adopt the policy when the threshold is crossed. By contrast, A’s mid-January threshold crossing activates the duty the same day; the fact that headcount was below 25 on the preceding 1 January does not supply a retroactive safe harbour.  

B, D and E either stayed below the threshold on every controlling measurement date or already satisfy the creation-and-distribution rules. Therefore A is the only employer clearly out of compliance on 2 January 2023.}

